  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1234 PM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nA large upper level ridge continues to amplify into the Midwest and \nGreat Lakes regions with the dry and very mild weather pattern \nexpected to last through this week. Surface high pressure will \nprevail with litle movement from where it is anchored over the \nAppalachian mountains. Southerly flow and increased WAA will funnel \nin much warmer air from the Gulf and southern US. There are no \nhazardous weather concerns this week as both high and low \ntemperatures will continue to climb well above seasonal averages. \nHigh temperatures will soar into the low to mid 60s starting \ntomorrow, which will be 15 to 20 degrees above normal for this \ntime of the year! Lows will be above freezing in the 40s. Friday\nand Saturday will be the warmest days of the week, with near \nrecord high temperatures.\n\nAs the ridge breaks down by late in the week, rain chances return on \nSunday as a cold front moves through. Compared to yesterday, models \nhave slowed down the timing of this front into our area by about 12 \nhours; wheras before it looked like we could get some scattered \nshowers Saturday night, now it appears like Saturday will stay dry \nand Sunday now has the bulk of the rain chances. By the time it \nmakes its way into our forecast area, the cold front should be \nfairly weak. With a limited amount of instability and potentially \nunfavorable morning timing to work with, only light rainfall amounts \nof 0.10-0.25\" are anticipated on Sunday. Have maintained low to \nmedium PoPs on Sunday with 20-40% chances. Following the frontal \npassage, temperatures will moderate back towards seasonable levels \nwith highs in the 40s. There are no real signs of winter yet as the \nmild weather pattern looks to hold at least through the end of \nmeteorological fall.",
